[00:36.98] [- Excerpt from the opening scene ("Before the law") of "The Trial" by Orson Welles, based on the novel by Franz Kafka -]
[02:48.58] The man growing childish in old age,
[02:51.18] He begs the very fleas
[02:53.34] To persuade the guard to change his mind and allow him to enter.
[02:56.76] His sight has dimmed,
[02:58.18] But in the darkness he perceives a radiance
[03:00.82] Streaming immortally from the door of the Law.
[03:04.58] And now, before he dies,
[03:08.54] All he's experienced condenses into one question, a question he's never asked.
[03:12.88] He beckons to the guard.
[03:14.34] Says the guard, "You are insatiable! What is it now?"
[03:17.16] Says the man, "Every man strives to attain the Law.
[03:22.72] How is it then that in all these years, no one else has ever come here, seeking admittance?"
[03:30.70] His hearing has failed, so the guard yells into his ear,
[03:34.90] "No one else but you could ever have obtained admittance!
[03:37.94] No one else could enter this door! This door was intended only for you!
